---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="Pool filter pressure too high? Causes and fixes | GK Swimming Pools"
  description="Pool filter pressure too high? See what normal PSI looks like, the common causes, and how we clean DE filters in Santa Clarita and the San Fernando Valley."
  canonical="https://gkswimmingpools.com/blog/pool-filter-pressure-high/"
>

  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "BlogPosting",
    "headline": "Pool Filter Pressure Too High? What It Means and How to Fix It",
    "description": "Pool filter pressure too high? See what normal PSI looks like, the common causes, and how we clean DE filters in Santa Clarita and the San Fernando Valley.",
    "datePublished": "2026-10-27",
    "author": { "@type": "LocalBusiness", "name": "GK Swimming Pools", "telephone": "(818) 804-8204" },
    "publisher": { "@type": "LocalBusiness", "name": "GK Swimming Pools", "url": "https://gkswimmingpools.com" },
    "image": "https://gkswimmingpools.com/images/blog/pool-filter-pressure-hero.webp",
    "mainEntityOfPage": { "@type": "WebPage", "@id": "https://gkswimmingpools.com/blog/pool-filter-pressure-high/" }
  })} />

  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "FAQPage",
    "mainEntity": [
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "What should my pool filter pressure be?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "There is no single right number, because every pool's plumbing is different. Most residential filters run somewhere between 10 and 25 PSI when clean. The number that matters is your own clean starting pressure. Write it down right after the filter is cleaned. When the gauge reads 8 to 10 PSI above that starting number, the filter needs attention."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Why is my filter pressure still high after backwashing?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "Backwashing only flushes the loose dirt and old DE. Oils from sunscreen and swimmers, plus calcium from hard water, can bake onto the filter grids, and backwashing won't remove them. If the pressure climbs right back up, the grids need to be taken out and cleaned by hand. A partly closed valve or a clog after the filter can also keep the pressure high."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "How often should a DE filter be cleaned?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "Backwash and add fresh DE whenever the pressure rises 8 to 10 PSI above your clean reading. On top of that, a DE filter should be fully taken apart and cleaned at least once or twice a year. Pools with heavy use, lots of trees, or hard water may need it more often. A filter that goes years without a full cleaning will struggle to keep the water clear."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "How much does pool filter cleaning cost?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "A full filter cleaning from GK Swimming Pools is $150. That includes taking the filter apart, cleaning the grids or cartridges, checking for torn grids and worn parts, and putting it back together with fresh DE. If we find damage, we show you what we found before replacing anything. We serve Santa Clarita and the San Fernando Valley."
        }
      }
    ]
  })} />

  <section class="blog-hero">
    <div class="blog-hero__bg">
      <img src="/images/blog/pool-filter-pressure-hero.webp"
        alt="DE filter grid assembly pulled out for a full pool filter cleaning by GK Swimming Pools"
        fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" width="1200" height="900" />
    </div>
    <div class="blog-hero__overlay"></div>
    <div class="container blog-hero__inner">
      <span class="eyebrow">Pool Repair Guide</span>
      <h1>Pool Filter Pressure Too High? What It Means and How to Fix It</h1>
      <div class="blog-meta">
        <span>GK Swimming Pools</span>
        <span class="blog-meta__dot"></span>
        <span>5 min read</span>
        <span class="blog-meta__dot"></span>
        <span>October 27, 2026</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <article class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="blog-body">

        <div class="blog-intro">
          <p>The pressure gauge on top of your filter is one of the easiest ways to tell how your pool is doing. When the needle climbs, your filter is telling you it's full. Here's what normal pressure looks like, what causes it to run high, what you can safely check, and how we clean a DE filter.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="blog-section">
          <span class="eyebrow">Know Your Numbers</span>
          <h2>What Your Pool Filter Pressure Should Be</h2>
          <p>There's no single correct number. Every pool's plumbing is a little different, so a clean filter might read 12 PSI on one pool and 20 on another. Most residential filters land somewhere between 10 and 25 PSI when they're clean.</p>
          <p>The number that matters is your own starting point. Right after your filter is cleaned, write down what the gauge reads. When it climbs <strong>8 to 10 PSI above that clean reading</strong>, it's time to clean the filter.</p>
          <p>Low pressure is a different problem. It usually means something is blocking water before it reaches the filter, like a full pump basket, a clogged skimmer, or a pump that's losing prime.</p>
        </div>

        <hr class="blog-divider"/>

        <div class="blog-section">
          <span class="eyebrow">Common Causes</span>
          <h2>Why Your Pool Filter Pressure Is High</h2>
          <p>High pressure almost always means water is having a hard time getting through the filter or past it. These are the causes we see most.</p>
          <ul class="blog-check-list">
            <li><span><strong>A dirty filter.</strong> This is the number one cause. As the filter traps dirt, there's less room for water to pass through, and the pressure rises.</span></li>
            <li><span><strong>Oils and calcium on the grids.</strong> Sunscreen, body oils, and hard water minerals coat the filter grids. Backwashing doesn't remove them, so the pressure stays high even after a backwash.</span></li>
            <li><span><strong>Too much DE.</strong> Adding more DE than your filter is sized for packs the grids too tight and pushes the pressure up right away.</span></li>
            <li><span><strong>A closed or partly closed valve.</strong> If a return valve or a valve after the filter is turned down, water backs up and the gauge climbs.</span></li>
            <li><span><strong>A clog after the filter.</strong> Debris stuck in the heater, a return line, or a chlorinator can block flow after the water leaves the filter.</span></li>
            <li><span><strong>An algae bloom.</strong> A pool full of algae loads up the filter fast. The pressure can jump in a day or two.</span></li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <hr class="blog-divider"/>

        <div class="blog-section">
          <span class="eyebrow">Check It Yourself</span>
          <h2>What You Can Safely Check Before You Call</h2>
          <p>These checks solve a lot of high pressure problems.</p>
          <ul class="blog-check-list">
            <li><span><strong>Valves.</strong> Make sure every valve after the filter and all the return valves are fully open.</span></li>
            <li><span><strong>Return jets.</strong> Feel the jets in the pool. Weak flow from all of them points to a dirty filter. Weak flow from one points to a clog in that line.</span></li>
            <li><span><strong>Backwash.</strong> If you have a DE filter and know how to backwash it, backwash and add the right amount of fresh DE. Then check the gauge again.</span></li>
            <li><span><strong>The gauge itself.</strong> With the pump off, the gauge should read zero. If it doesn't, the gauge is bad and needs to be replaced.</span></li>
          </ul>

          <div class="callout-warn">
            <p>Never open a filter while it's under pressure. Turn the pump off at the breaker, then open the air relief valve on top of the filter and let the pressure out before you touch the clamp or lid. A filter lid that comes off under pressure can cause serious injury. If you're not sure, leave it to a professional.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <hr class="blog-divider"/>

        <div class="blog-section">
          <span class="eyebrow">Why It Matters</span>
          <h2>What Happens When You Ignore a Dirty Filter</h2>
          <p>A dirty filter can't keep up. Water moves slower, less of it gets filtered, and chlorine has a harder time doing its job. That's how a pool that looks fine one week turns <a href="/blog/cloudy-pool-water/">cloudy</a> or green the next.</p>

          <div class="blog-tip">
            <div class="blog-tip__label">From a Recent Job</div>
            <p>We had a client who hadn't cleaned their filter in almost two years. The pool looked good, but only because nobody was using it. Then they had one pool party, and the next thing they knew they had an algae bloom. When we opened the filter, the inside smelled like an aquarium. We cleaned the filter, and the pool was as good as new.</p>
          </div>

          <p>Restricted flow also strains the rest of your equipment. Your pump works harder to push water through, and a heater needs good flow to fire at all. If your <a href="/blog/pool-heater-not-working/">pool heater won't turn on</a> or your <a href="/blog/swimming-pool-pump-repair/">pump is running loud</a>, a dirty filter is one of the first things we check.</p>
        </div>

        <hr class="blog-divider"/>

        <div class="blog-section">
          <span class="eyebrow">Our Process</span>
          <h2>How We Clean a DE Filter</h2>
          <p>DE filters are the most common type we see in the San Fernando Valley and Santa Clarita. A full cleaning goes beyond a backwash.</p>

          <div class="step-list">
            <div class="step-item">
              <div class="step-item__num">1</div>
              <div class="step-item__body">
                <h3>Shut down and release pressure</h3>
                <p>We turn off the pump, open the air relief valve, and drain the filter tank before opening anything.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="step-item">
              <div class="step-item__num">2</div>
              <div class="step-item__body">
                <h3>Pull the grids</h3>
                <p>We take the filter apart and remove the grid assembly so every grid can be cleaned and inspected.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="step-item">
              <div class="step-item__num">3</div>
              <div class="step-item__body">
                <h3>Clean every grid</h3>
                <p>We hose off the old DE and dirt and treat the grids for oils and calcium buildup that a backwash leaves behind.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="step-item">
              <div class="step-item__num">4</div>
              <div class="step-item__body">
                <h3>Inspect for damage</h3>
                <p>We check for torn grids, cracked manifolds, and worn o-rings. A torn grid lets DE blow back into the pool, so we catch it here.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="step-item">
              <div class="step-item__num">5</div>
              <div class="step-item__body">
                <h3>Reassemble and add fresh DE</h3>
                <p>We put the filter back together, start the pump, and add the right amount of fresh DE for your filter's size.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="step-item">
              <div class="step-item__num">6</div>
              <div class="step-item__body">
                <h3>Record your clean pressure</h3>
                <p>We note the new clean reading so you know exactly where the gauge should sit and when it's time for the next cleaning.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <hr class="blog-divider"/>

        <div class="blog-section">
          <span class="eyebrow">Next Steps</span>
          <h2>Need Your Pool Filter Cleaned?</h2>
          <p>A full filter cleaning from GK Swimming Pools is $150. We're a family-run company, three generations in, serving the San Fernando Valley and Santa Clarita. If we find a torn grid or a worn part, we'll show you what we found before we replace anything. If you're on our <a href="/services/pool-maintenance/">weekly pool service</a>, we keep an eye on your filter every visit and let you know when it's due for a cleaning.</p>

          <div class="blog-cta-inline">
            <a href="/services/pool-repairs" class="btn btn--primary">Pool Repair Services</a>
            <a href="tel:8188048204" class="btn btn--outline">(818) 804-8204</a>
          </div>
        </div>

      </div>
    </div>
  </article>

  <div class="cta-band">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>Filter Pressure Climbing?</h2>
      <p>We'll clean your filter, check it for damage, and get your water moving again. Serving the San Fernando Valley and Santa Clarita.</p>
      <a href="/contact" class="btn btn--primary">Get In Touch</a>
    </div>
  </div>

</Layout>

<style>
  .blog-hero { position:relative; height:460px; overflow:hidden; display:flex; align-items:flex-end; }
  .blog-hero__bg { position:absolute; inset:0; }
  .blog-hero__bg img { width:100%; height:100%; object-fit:cover; object-position:center 35%; display:block; }
  .blog-hero__overlay { position:absolute; inset:0; background:linear-gradient(to top, rgba(6,47,97,.90) 0%, rgba(6,47,97,.35) 60%, rgba(6,47,97,.12) 100%); }
  .blog-hero__inner { position:relative; z-index:2; padding-bottom: var(--space-12); }
  .blog-hero__inner .eyebrow { color:var(--color-aqua); margin-bottom: var(--space-3); }
  .blog-hero__inner h1 { color:var(--color-white); font-size:clamp(1.7rem,3.6vw,2.6rem); margin-bottom: var(--space-4); max-width:820px; }
  .blog-meta { display:flex; align-items:center; gap: var(--space-3); color:rgba(255,255,255,.72); font-size:var(--text-sm); font-weight:600; flex-wrap:wrap; }
  .blog-meta__dot { width:4px; height:4px; border-radius:50%; background:var(--color-aqua); }

  .blog-body { max-width:760px; margin:0 auto; }
  .blog-intro { background:var(--color-mist); border-left:4px solid var(--color-aqua); padding:var(--space-6) var(--space-8); margin-bottom:var(--space-12); border-radius:0 var(--radius-sm) var(--radius-sm) 0; }
  .blog-intro p { font-size:var(--text-lg); color:var(--color-deep); font-weight:600; line-height:1.7; margin:0; }
  .blog-section { margin-bottom:var(--space-12); }
  .blog-section .eyebrow { margin-bottom: var(--space-3); }
  .blog-section h2 { margin-bottom: var(--space-6); }
  .blog-section p { color:var(--color-muted); line-height:1.85; margin-bottom: var(--space-4); }
  .blog-section p a { color:var(--color-deep); font-weight:600; text-decoration:underline; text-underline-offset:3px; }
  .blog-section strong { color:var(--color-deep); }
  .blog-check-list { list-style:none; margin: var(--space-4) 0; display:flex; flex-direction:column; gap: var(--space-3); }
  .blog-check-list li { display:flex; align-items:flex-start; gap: var(--space-3); font-size:var(--text-base); color:var(--color-muted); line-height:1.7; }
  .blog-check-list li::before { content:"\2713"; color:var(--color-aqua); font-weight:800; font-size:1rem; flex-shrink:0; margin-top:2px; }
  .blog-divider { border:none; border-top:2px solid var(--color-border); margin:var(--space-12) 0; }

  /* REPAIR STEPS */
  .step-list { display:flex; flex-direction:column; gap:var(--space-3); margin:var(--space-6) 0; }
  .step-item {
    display:flex; gap:var(--space-4); align-items:flex-start;
    background:var(--color-white); border:1.5px solid var(--color-border);
    border-radius:var(--radius-sm); padding:var(--space-4) var(--space-6);
  }
  .step-item__num {
    flex-shrink:0; width:38px; height:38px; border-radius:50%;
    background:var(--color-aqua); color:var(--color-deep);
    font-family:var(--font-heading); font-weight:800; font-size:1.05rem;
    display:flex; align-items:center; justify-content:center; line-height:1;
  }
  .step-item__body h3 {
    font-family:var(--font-heading); font-weight:700; font-size:var(--text-base);
    color:var(--color-deep); text-transform:uppercase; letter-spacing:.03em;
    margin-bottom:var(--space-2);
  }
  .step-item__body p { font-size:var(--text-sm); color:var(--color-muted); line-height:1.7; margin:0; }

  /* WARNING CALLOUT */
  .callout-warn {
    background:var(--color-white); border:1.5px solid var(--color-border);
    border-left:3px solid #e8a33d; border-radius:var(--radius-sm);
    padding:var(--space-6); margin:var(--space-6) 0;
  }
  .callout-warn p { font-size:var(--text-sm); color:var(--color-muted); line-height:1.8; margin:0; }

  .blog-tip { background:var(--color-deep); border-radius:var(--radius-sm); padding: var(--space-6) var(--space-8); margin: var(--space-8) 0; }
  .blog-tip__label { font-size:var(--text-xs); font-weight:700; letter-spacing:.15em; text-transform:uppercase; color:var(--color-aqua); margin-bottom: var(--space-2); font-family:var(--font-heading); }
  .blog-tip p { color:rgba(255,255,255,.88); margin:0; }

  .blog-cta-inline { display:flex; gap: var(--space-4); flex-wrap:wrap; margin-top: var(--space-8); }
  .btn--outline { display:inline-block; border:2px solid var(--color-deep); color:var(--color-deep); font-family:var(--font-heading); font-weight:700; font-size:var(--text-sm); letter-spacing:.06em; text-transform:uppercase; padding: var(--space-4) var(--space-8); border-radius:var(--radius-sm); text-decoration:none; }

  @media (max-width:640px) {
    .blog-hero { height:360px; }
    .blog-intro { padding: var(--space-4); }
    .step-item { gap:var(--space-4); padding:var(--space-4); }
    .blog-cta-inline { flex-direction:column; }
  }
</style>
